This file is informational for LLM agents that are directed here. If you have not been specifically directed to work on tasks within this to-do directory, then there's nothing for you to do here. Unless you're an agent who has been specifically directed to edit this file, this file itself, this README, is only for revision by humans. However, the individual files are editable by agents and should be edited, in accordance with the instructions here. If you *have* directed here by a prompt, then the general format for working with this directory is:

Each text file is sort of like a GitHub issue or a Jira ticket. These should be tracked and pushed to remote on a regular basis. This is unlike all other code, which is only pushed to remote on a specific instruction. The intent here is to allow me to review and edit to-do tickets on my phone / remotely as I am testing the app, which is usually with my phone. 

If the ticket says "==CLOSED==" at the top then it's a closed ticket and there's nothing to do, move on. 

When they're originally instantiated, tickets will just have text from human (or an LLM acting at the direction of a human) about an issue that needs to be solved. 

You may see also see some with evidence that an agent has worked on a fix before (Such as the instructions below having been followed). If that's all you see, then it seems like an agent tried to fix/do it and no human has looked at it yet, so just leave it alone. However, you see "==reply==" at the end, then more text, then that's another reply from a human, either instructing you to close it out or work further. 

Unless otherwise instructed by a prompt: 
(1) determine all open tickets in the directory first. try to do the easiest, simplest, least failure-prone open tickets first (so higher-confidence commits are earlier in the history); 
(2) Work until you think you have a successful fix that builds, like you would with any ordinary prompt;
(3) Put the text "==Original Issue==" at the top of the before the original issue text (if not done already)
(4) If a ticket seems so unclear that you feel like there are really uncertain things or that the user doesn't understand what they're asking for, it is acceptable to not try to solve the ticket, but merely leave a message like you would with plan mode. Just be clear that that's what you're doing. Leaving a potential plan for the user or questions because the issue seems so poorly written, you're not sure what they want, or that what they want might have unintended consequences. But if you're greater than 90% sure that you can implement it and a user will be fine with your implementation without additional feedback, then go ahead and make minor decisions on your own to implement and solve. When you are doing a pass through tickets, the user is not going to be there for interactive prompts, so just pick one: either do it all non-interactively (if reasonably confident can resolve) or write it out to the ticket as a plan for the user to review later (if not confident / contradictions / user unclear or apparently missing important info).
(5) Put "==Status Report: [human-readable-date-time-California-time]==" with your analysis (if too uncertain) or completion status report (if a fix/implentation attempt) for your resolution attempt: what you changed, how, any testing or things you want the human to think about. "[human-readable-date-time-California-time]" is of course not a literal, but something for you to fill in with the date-time of your attempt completion report
(6) For implementation attempts, do a local commit after successful build.
(7) Closed tickets should have "-CLOSED" appended to the end of their file name and moved to the "closed" subdirectory. Files in that directory can be ignored by agents unless specifically directed. 
(8) Remember that these tickets are going into a public repository, so make sure that they are scrubbed of any PII or information that seems like it should not be public. Report that back into your main chat output.